% ============================================================
% ABSTRACT
% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
 oindent\textbf{Context}: Automated verdict classification is valuable for
computing-education platforms, yet most approaches require source-code access
or costly LLM inference. Competitive programming platforms return both a
structured verdict and rich execution metadata for every submission,
making it possible to ask: what verdict information is recoverable from
metadata alone, and where does metadata become insufficient?

 oindent\textbf{Objective}: We systematically compare traditional ML and LLMs
for metadata-based verdict classification to characterize the
platform-encoding boundary---the point at which verdict information is
deterministically encoded by the platform's own measurement thresholds
rather than by features that generalize across platforms.

 oindent\textbf{Method}: 13,360 Codeforces submissions across five verdict types. Processed metadata features extracted via the Codeforces REST API; raw submission payloads are retained locally and are not redistributed per platform Terms of Service---see Section~\ref{sec:ethics} for ethics details. We evaluate Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and Logistic Regression (seven metadata features) plus two LLM configurations (DeepSeek-V3 and \texttt{Qwen2.5:3B}, using five metadata fields as text input), under identical zero-shot protocols. Significance is assessed with McNemar's test with Bonferroni correction. For a fair ML--LLM comparison, we also report Gradient Boosting trained on the same five features available to the LLMs.

 oindent\textbf{Results}: Three verdict types are platform-encoded: CE
(compile-time = 0 ms), TLE (execution time = limit), and MLE (memory = limit)
are deterministically recoverable from execution metadata (F1 $\ge$0.89)
because these values are defined by the platform's own measurement thresholds.
These three verdict types account for 18.4\% of all submissions in our dataset,
meaning automated classification can immediately deliver actionable feedback for these
platform-encoded verdicts without requiring instructors' manual review, where GB achieves 95.02\% overall accuracy (BAcc=0.89). The WA$\leftrightarrow$RE boundary is not separable from metadata alone (RE F1 = 0.52; all models fail here). 
DeepSeek-V3 reaches 76.65\% on valid
predictions (0.8\% invalid) but collapses on RE (F1 = 0.03), while \texttt{Qwen2.5:3B}
achieves only 35.50\% (10.1\% invalid). This comparison is asymmetric:
ML models were trained on 10,688 labeled samples while LLMs received no task-specific
training. Supervised ML (7-feature GB: 95.02\% overall accuracy, balanced accuracy = 0.89) outperforms DeepSeek-V3 zero-shot
(76.65\%) by 18.4 pp; a fair-feature comparison using GB trained on the same five
features as the LLMs (92.0\%) still outperforms DeepSeek-V3 by 15.4 pp.

 oindent\textbf{Conclusions}: We characterize the platform-encoding boundary for verdict classification from submission metadata. CE, TLE, and MLE are directly recoverable from platform thresholds (F1 $\ge$0.89), while WA$\leftrightarrow$RE is not metadata-separable (RE F1 = 0.52). These findings delineate where metadata alone becomes insufficient, motivating source-code analysis as the natural next step, and provide empirical grounding for lightweight metadata-based verdict screening---classroom validation required before drawing conclusions about learning impact. From a pedagogical perspective, the three platform-encoded verdicts (CE, TLE, MLE) are precisely those where immediate, specific feedback is most actionable; we hypothesize that a student receiving a CE verdict would benefit from syntax guidance rather than algorithmic review---a claim that remains to be validated in a classroom study. We release the dataset, pipeline,
and deployment guidelines at \url{https://github.com/huwenbin123huwenbin/programming-error-classification}.

\end{abstract}

