\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Finding 1: The Platform-Encoding Boundary Precisely Demarcates Feasible Feedback}

\paragraph{Addressing the circularity concern.} The platform-encoding boundary does not mean that CE/TLE/MLE classification is trivial. A classifier must learn from labeled data that \texttt{execution\_time}=0 predicts CE---this requires generalization, not memorization of threshold values. Our ablation study (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) confirms this: while execution time is the dominant feature (8.8\,pp drop), removing it does not reduce accuracy to zero, indicating the model also leverages complementary signals (\texttt{user\_success\_rate}, \texttt{problem\_rating}) to distinguish CE from near-CE boundary cases.

Submission metadata enables near-perfect recovery of CE, TLE, MLE because the platform's resource measurements operationally
define these outcomes. This is not a classifier result---it is a platform design property.
The critical finding is the WA$\leftrightarrow$RE boundary: neither ML
(GB RE F1 = 0.52) nor LLMs (DeepSeek-V3 RE F1 = 0.03) reliably distinguish
these classes from metadata alone. This boundary is precisely where pedagogical
feedback is most needed, suggesting that source-code integration or human expert review might be warranted.
Gradient Boosting achieves 95.02\% accuracy by leveraging the CE/TLE/MLE signal;
DeepSeek-V3 (76.05\%) and \texttt{Qwen2.5:3B} (31.92\%) do not close this gap.
ML inference cost ($0.01/1k) is negligible versus DeepSeek-V3 ($0.10/1k).
\subsection{Finding 2: Execution Time Dominance and Feature Insights}

Execution time (\texttt{time\_consumed\_ms}) is the dominant predictor:
removing it reduces accuracy by 8.8 pp. This confirms that runtime behavior---
not problem metadata---carries the metadata-derivable signal for CE, TLE, and MLE.
Memory consumption follows (1.40 pp), user success rate contributes 3.6 pp;
problem rating, language, and problem type each contribute 1--2 pp, while
submission hour contributes negligibly (0.0 pp). The WA$\leftrightarrow$RE
ambiguity remains irreducible from metadata, suggesting that source-code features might be required.
Despite lower accuracy, LLMs offer explainability advantages~\cite{finnie2022robots}; a hybrid strategy (ML for classification, LLM for explanation) leverages both.

\subsection{Finding 3: Error Type Skew and RE Challenges}

The class imbalance (WA 76.6\% vs.\ MLE 1.5\%) and low RE F1-score (0.52) highlight limitations. RE encompasses diverse root causes that metadata cannot distinguish, suggesting that source-code features might help. Prior work~\cite{altadmri2015most} confirms RE difficulty as a general limitation of metadata-only approaches.

\subsection{LLM Error Analysis}

We evaluate two LLM configurations under identical zero-shot protocol: DeepSeek-V3 (API) and \texttt{Qwen2.5:3B} (local). Their 44.1pp accuracy gap (76.05\% vs.\ 31.92\%) shows model scale matters.

\textbf{DeepSeek-V3} achieves 76.65\% on valid predictions (0.8\% invalid), recovering execution-bounded verdicts (WA=0.85, TLE=0.84) but collapsing on RE (F1=0.03). This confirms the WA$\leftrightarrow$RE distinction is not recoverable from metadata alone, even for frontier LLMs.

\textbf{\texttt{Qwen2.5:3B}} achieves only 35.50\% (10.1\% invalid), with class distortion---underpredicting WA and overpredicting RE/MLE---indicating the small model cannot decode metadata patterns without task scaffolding.

\textit{Implications}: Zero-shot prompting with metadata is insufficient for large-scale verdict classification; local small models are not viable substitutes for online APIs. Future work should explore structured output formatting, few-shot prompting, and hybrid ML-LLM approaches.

\textit{Why Pilot Results Were Misleadingly High}: The 207-sample pilot was
selected from the same population as the training set (Codeforces Round
2237--2240, rating 800--3500), and could have overrepresented straightforward
cases where metadata signals are strong (e.g., CE with zero execution time,
MLE with high memory consumption).

